REVIEW=[
 ('i','If f(t) = 3t² + 4t − 5 then f′(t) is',['3t² − 4t + 5','6t + 4','12','6t − 5'],'b',['Apply the power rule to 3t² to obtain 6t.','The derivative of 4t is 4 and the constant −5 has derivative zero.','Thus f′(t) = 6t + 4.','Option (b) is correct.']),
 ('ii','If f(t) = 3t² + 4t − 5 then f″(t) is',['3t² − 4t + 5','6t − 4','6','6t + 5'],'c',['First differentiate to obtain f′(t) = 6t + 4.','Differentiate again: f″(t) = 6.','The second derivative is constant; the other three options retain t.','Option (c) is correct.']),
 ('iii',r'If y = eᵐˣ then \(d^ny/dx^n\) equals',[r'\(me^{nx}\log(e^x)\)',r'\(m^ne^{mx}\)',r'\(me^{nx}\)',r'\(me^xe^{mx}\)'],'b',['Treat m as a constant and n as a nonnegative integer.','Each differentiation of eᵐˣ supplies the chain factor m.','After n differentiations, the factor is mⁿ and the original exponential remains.','Option (b), mⁿeᵐˣ, is correct.']),
 ('iv','The fifth derivative of f(x) = eˣ is',[r'\(e^x\)',r'\(e^{x+5}\)',r'\(5e^x\)',r'\(e^{5x}\)'],'a',['The derivative of eˣ equals eˣ.','Repeating the same differentiation five times leaves the function unchanged.','Neither its argument nor its coefficient is multiplied by five.','Option (a) is correct.']),
 ('v','The fourth derivative of sinx is',[r'\(d^4y/dx^4=\sin x\)',r'\(d^4y/dx^4=\cos x\)',r'\(d^4y/dx^4=-\sin x\)',r'\(d^4y/dx^4=-\cos x\)'],'a',['The first derivative is cosx.','The next two are −sinx and −cosx.','The fourth returns to sinx, completing the four-step cycle.','Option (a) is correct.']),
 ('vi',r'If f(x) and its derivatives at x = x₀ are f′(x₀), f″(x₀), … fⁿ(x), then the nth order polynomial f(x) will be equal to:',[r'\(f(x)+f(x_0-x)f′(x)+...+\frac{(x_0-x)^n}{n!}f^n(x)\)',r'\(f(x_0)+(x-x_0)f′(x)+...+\frac{(x-x_0)^n}{n!}f^n(x_0)\)',r'\(f(x+h)+(x+h)f′(h)+...+\frac{x-h}{n!}f^n(h)\)',r'\(f(x-h)+(x-h)f′(h)+...+\frac{x-h}{n!}f^n(h)\)'],'b — intended, after correcting f′(x) to f′(x₀)',['The nth Taylor polynomial about x₀ has coefficients evaluated at x₀, not at variable x.','Its standard form is pₙ(x) = f(x₀) + f′(x₀)(x − x₀) + … + f⁽ⁿ⁾(x₀)(x − x₀)ⁿ/n!.','The key chooses (b), the intended Taylor form, but its printed linear coefficient is f′(x), which needs correction to f′(x₀).','A finite Taylor polynomial generally approximates f(x); equality is exact only in special cases or after including a suitable remainder.','No printed option is fully correct as written for a general function. Original choices remain in the question.']),
 ('vii','To calculate the first five terms of Taylor’s series for f(x) = e²ˣ, the MAPLE command is',[r'taylor(x,e^(2*x)=0)','taylor(e^(2*x))','taylor(e^(2*x),x=0,5)','taylor(e^(2*x),x=5)'],'c',['The expression must be the first argument and the expansion variable/point the second.','The order 5 requests powers below x⁵ about zero.','The terms are 1 + 2x + 2x² + 4x³/3 + 2x⁴/3 + O(x⁵).','Option (c) specifies the expression, zero expansion point and required order. These are manually checked expected series terms.']),
 ('viii','The equation of normal at point (x₀,y₀) is',[r'\(x-x_0=\frac1m(y-y_0)\)',r'\(y-y_0=\frac1m(x_0-x)\)',r'\(y-y_0=m(x-x_0)\)',r'\(y-y_0=-\frac1m(x-x_0)\)'],'d — also b',['For a finite nonzero tangent slope m, the normal slope is −1/m.','Point–slope form gives y − y₀ = −(x − x₀)/m, option (d).','Option (b) uses x₀ − x = −(x − x₀), so it is the same normal equation.','Both (b) and (d) are correct for m ≠ 0; the key selects (d). If m = 0, use the vertical normal x = x₀ instead of dividing by zero.']),
 ('ix','The angle of intersection of two curves can be calculated using',[r'\(\theta=\sin^{-1}\frac{1+m_1m_2}{1-m_1m_2}\)',r'\(\theta=\tan^{-1}\frac{1-m_1m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\)',r'\(\theta=\tan^{-1}\frac{m_1-m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\)',r'\(\theta=\sin^{-1}\frac{m_1+m_2}{1+m_1m_2}\)'],'c',['The tangent subtraction identity gives tan(α − β) = (tanα − tanβ)/(1 + tanα tanβ).','With tangent slopes m₁,m₂ this gives the source option (c).','For the smaller nonnegative angle, use the absolute value of the ratio.','If 1 + m₁m₂ = 0, the lines are perpendicular; the angle is π/2 and the fraction is not defined.']),
 ('x','If f is differentiable on (a,b), it is strictly increasing if',['f(x) > 0','f′(x) > 0','f″(x) > 0','f″(x) < 0'],'b',['Positive first derivative throughout the interval means positive secant slopes by the mean value theorem.','Therefore x₁ < x₂ implies f(x₁) < f(x₂).','A positive function value does not imply increasing, and a second derivative sign describes concavity.','Option (b) is the stated sufficient condition.'])
]
HISTORY='Omer Khayyam was a Persian mathematician, Astronomer and philosopher. He was born in Nishapur in north eastern Iran. He was most notable person in the history of mathematics because of his work on the classification and solution of cubic equation. Where he proved the geometric solution by the intersection of conics. He also contributed to the understanding of the parallel axiom. As an astronomer he designed the Jalali calendar, a Solor calander. He was the first person, who considered the three cases of acute, right and obtuse angle for summit angles of a Khayyam saccheri quadrilateral, three cases which are exhaustive and pairwise mutually exclusive.'
PAGES=[page('068_page_2.jpg',128,[heading('Review Exercise 4'),heading('1. Choose the correct option.')]+[paragraph(num+'. '+q+' '+' '.join('('+letter+') '+opt for letter,opt in zip('abcd',options))) for num,q,options,answer,steps in REVIEW]+[note('Item (vi) has a printed Taylor-coefficient typo and no fully correct general formula as written; the key-intended (b) is explained with its correction. Item (viii) has two equivalent correct options (b),(d); both are identified.')]),
 page('069_page_1.jpg',129,[heading('Summary'),paragraph('The second derivative of y = f(x) can be written with any of the following notations:'),eq(r'\frac{d^2y}{dx^2},\ y″,\ f″(x),\ D_x^2[f(x)]'),paragraph('The third derivative can be written in a similar way. For n ≥ 4, the nth derivative is written as f⁽ⁿ⁾(x).'),
 paragraph('The second derivative of parametric functions x(t) and y(t) can be found as follows:'),eq(r'\frac{d^2y}{dx^2}=\frac{d}{dt}\left(\frac{\frac{dy}{dt}}{\frac{dx}{dt}}\right)\frac{dt}{dx}=\frac{\frac{dx}{dt}\frac{d^2y}{dt^2}-\frac{dy}{dt}\frac{d^2x}{dt^2}}{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^3}'),eq(r'\frac{dt}{dx}=\frac1{\frac{dx}{dt}}'),
 paragraph('The popular notation for Taylor theorem of order n is:'),eq(r'f(x_0+h)=f(x_0)+f′(x_0)h+f″(x_0)\frac{h^2}{2!}+...+f^{(n)}(x_0)\frac{h^n}{n!}+...'),paragraph('The popular notation for Maclaurin’s theorem of order n is:'),eq(r'f(x_0+h)=f(0)+f′(0)h+f″(0)\frac{h^2}{2!}+...+f^{(n)}(0)\frac{h^n}{n!}+...'),
 paragraph('i. If two lines are parallel, then their slopes are equal. ii. If two lines are perpendicular, then the product of their slopes equals −1. iii. The tangent equation at a point P(x₀,y₀) is (y − y₀) = m(x − x₀). iv. The normal equation at P(x₀,y₀) is (y − y₀) = −(1/m)(x − x₀).'),
 paragraph('If f(x) is differentiable on (a,b), then f(x) is strictly increasing on (a,b) if f′(x) > 0 for a < x < b; strictly decreasing on (a,b) if f′(x) < 0 for a < x < b.'),paragraph('If a continuous function f(x) has a relative extremum at c, then c must be a critical value of f(x).'),
 paragraph('The graph of f(x) is concave upward on (a,b) where f″(x) > 0, and concave downward where f″(x) < 0.'),paragraph('If y = f(x) is continuous on (a,b) and has an inflection point at x = c, then either f″(c) = 0 or f″(c) does not exist.'),
 paragraph('i. A point P(c,f(c)) on the graph of a differential function y = f(x) where the concavity changes is called a point of inflection. ii. If a function has a point of inflection P(c,f(c)) at a partition c and it is possible to differentiate the function twice, then f″(c) = 0.'),
 note('The summary’s Maclaurin offset left side should be f(h), because the expansion point is 0. A finite Taylor expression is an approximation unless its remainder vanishes; an infinite series needs convergence to the function. Parametric division requires dx/dt ≠ 0. Perpendicular slope products and −1/m apply to finite nonzero slopes; handle vertical lines separately. A zero second derivative is a candidate, not a sufficient test, for an inflection: the concavity must actually change.'),
 heading('History'),paragraph(HISTORY),figure('omar-khayyam-source129','Omar Khayyam (1048–1131)','Original source portrait of Omar Khayyam, associated only with this history box.'),note('The historical paragraph retains the source spelling and wording, including Omer in the text and Omar in the portrait caption.')
 ])]
